{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 2 complete — RED→GREEN, mutation-checked, landed for review\n\n**Fix (restoration, not creation).** `compact_trigger._canonical_tracker` (new, lazy-import,\nnever-raises) resolves the tracker through symlinks via `_store.lock.canonical_tracker`, and\n`_rebar_dir` now derives from the resolved path — so all three sidecars land on the CANONICAL\nstore's `.rebar/` and no caller can defeat the invariant. Second site in the same file:\n`_spawn_detached_sweep` hands the detached child the canonical tracker (that argument also\nbecomes `run_sweep`'s `repo_root`, and the child outlives the worktree that spawned it; its\n`cwd` was already resolved by `_proc.detached_child_cwd` for exactly this reason).\n\nNo new lock, no new protocol, no config key, no new caller-visible failure mode, no new\ndependency, no weakened test. The lock stays non-blocking/best-effort.\n\n**Contention analysis for the many-worktree case (~100 worktrees on this host).** The lock now\nactually excludes, so the question is whether that introduces waiting. It does not: `_acquire_\ntrigger_lock` is `O_CREAT|O_EXCL` non-blocking and a loser simply returns `None`, so\n`maybe_compact` declines to spawn and `run_sweep` returns — no blocking, no queue, no deadlock.\nThe change strictly REDUCES concurrent work: previously N worktrees could each run a store-wide\nsweep against one store; now one does and the rest stand aside. The shared stamp removes the\nredundant-sweep storm outright. No caller assumed the trigger was cheap-because-per-process —\nthe trigger's cost is the same two `O(1)` checks, and the only newly-shared cost is one `stat`\non a different path. The one new failure mode considered and rejected as a concern: a leaked\nlock now suppresses the trigger store-wide rather than per-worktree — but that lock already\ncarries the v2 ownership stamp and is reclaimed by `lock_owner.stamped_file_is_stale`, the\nexact discipline `knavish-stimulated-bluebottle` added, and `test_a_stale_worker_lock_is_\nreclaimed` covers it.\n\n**RED→GREEN.** Five new tests in `tests/unit/test_compact_trigger.py`, authored BEFORE the fix\nand held out of the fixer's tree (stashed to a patch; the fixer was given only the contract\ncard and told tests/ was off limits, and it verified against its own scratch script):\npath invariance across two worktree views; real cross-worktree lock contention; stamp written\nin A read in B (with a negative control asserting B reads stale beforehand); sidecars never\nwritten into the worktree; detached child handed the canonical tracker. All five observed RED\nfor the right reasons first, all five GREEN after.\n\n**Per-site mutation check — both hunks independently load-bearing.**\n* Re-seed site 1 only (`_rebar_dir` loses the resolution): 4 failed, 15 passed — the argv test\n  stayed green, so site 2 is not what makes those four pass.\n* Re-seed site 2 only (argv back to the raw tracker): 1 failed, 18 passed — only the argv test\n  went red, so site 1 is not what makes it pass.\n* Fix restored: 19 passed.\n\n**Replication of the original report.** The Phase-1 probe, re-run unchanged against the fixed\ncode: lock paths from both worktree views now equal and canonical, `paths equal: True`; the\nsecond acquire returns `None`; `stale in B after A swept: False`; the log path is the canonical\nstore's. Both worktrees' `.rebar` stay empty; the canonical `.rebar` holds the lock.\n\n**Gates.** `make lint` (ruff check + format-check, complexity ratchet `active=49 new=0\nincreased=0 stale=0`, config/env/DCO/criteria/skill gates, zizmor, actionlint) green;\n`make typecheck` 616 files clean; the five repo-wide CI oracles the fast gates skip\n(`check_wall_clock_asserts`, `check_raw_git_writes`, `check_comment_hygiene`,\n`check_docs_index`, `check_config_reads`) all OK; full unit sweep **12077 passed, 44 skipped**.\n\n**Step-7 sweeps.**\n* *Construct axis.* Grepping the whole of `src/` for a tracker-derived `.rebar` sidecar finds\n  exactly two other sites — `llm/enrich_drain.py:42` and `llm/overlap/queue.py:259` — both\n  already fixed by the sibling `nuclear-calm-heron` change and both off-limits to this lane, so\n  nothing left to fix. Every other `.rebar` path in the tree is `repo_root`-derived config\n  (prompts, overlays, caches), which is legitimately per-clone and not a store sidecar.\n* *Parity axis.* Rule: an artifact coordinating the ONE shared store must be keyed on the\n  canonical store. Census — CLI `rebar compact-all` → `compact_all_cli` → `record_sweep`\n  (`compact.py:617-622`): HOLDS via the fixed core. Scheduled workflow\n  `.github/workflows/compact-sweep.yml` → same CLI: HOLDS. Close path\n  `transition_close.py:358-360` → `maybe_compact`: HOLDS. Library `rebar.compact` /\n  MCP `compact_ticket` → `compact_cli`, per-ticket and never a sweep (`compact_cli([])` is a\n  usage error, so no surface can sweep without stamping): N/A. Read side (`_sweep_is_stale`,\n  the storm-control probe): HOLDS by construction. One MISSING slot — `doctor_locks` has no\n  `compact-worker` leg — filed as `bemused-painted-worm` (`8a0d-96ca-a60e-4da6`), linked\n  `discovered_from` this bug, deliberately not fixed here because adding a doctor leg is new\n  capability rather than restoration.\n\n**Residual risk.** None observed beyond the doctor-leg gap above (ticketed). The stale-lock\nblast radius change is analysed above and accepted: it is the intended consequence of the lock\nbecoming store-scoped, and it is already guarded by the shared staleness table.\n\n**Also updated:** `docs/concurrency.md` now states that the worker lock, the sweep stamp and\nthe worker log live in the canonical store's `.rebar/`, so the doc no longer reads as if a\nper-clone `.rebar` were correct.\n\n**Gerrit change:** https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1994 (commit `a6a875d268`)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787311263058788001,"uuid":"2781010f-d699-4bea-af94-f26036bf6847"}